    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "You can now close a job, and once it's closed no new invoice can be raised on it. `bin/e2e` builds and runs, and the acceptance test passes.\n\n**Database:** a new migration, `V59__project_closed.sql`, adds a `closed` column to jobs. Existing jobs start as open.\n\n**Server:** the server is actually Spring Boot, not OfficeFloor as the request said, so I followed the Spring conventions in `CLAUDE.md`.\n- `POST /api/projects/{id}/close` closes a job and records `PROJECT_CLOSED id=\u2026` in the audit log. Closing a job that's already closed does nothing.\n- Job responses now include `closed`.\n- The server refuses to create an invoice on a closed job (HTTP 409). This is enforced on the server too, so it holds even if someone bypasses the UI.\n- The test setup endpoint can now seed jobs as already closed (`closed: true`).\n\n**Front end:**\n- The job page shows `OPEN` or `CLOSED` (`project-status`) and a \"Close job\" button (`project-close`) while the job is open.\n- On a closed job, the \"New invoice\" button (`invoice-new`) and the new-invoice form are disabled, with a short note explaining why (`invoice-closed-note`).\n\n**Things to know:**\n- Closing is separate from the existing Active / On hold / Finished status, and changing that status doesn't close or reopen a job.\n- There is no way to reopen a closed job, since the request didn't ask for one. If you want it, it's a small addition.\n- The block applies only to creating invoices. Invoices that already exist can still be edited, sent and cancelled after the job is closed.",
